@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -uo pipefail
+
+if (( $# < 2 )); then
+  echo "usage: $0 LOG COMMAND [ARG ...]" >&2
+  exit 2
+fi
+
+log=$1
+shift
+
+{
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+  printf 'WORKDIR: %s\n' "$PWD"
+} >"$log"
+
+"$@" > >(tee -a "$log") 2> >(tee -a "$log" >&2)
+status=$?
+printf 'EXIT_STATUS: %d\n' "$status" | tee -a "$log"
+exit "$status"

codex
All substantive gates are complete. The proof is cleanly reconstructible, result-sensitive, body-sensitive, full-domain over finite integer lists, and pinned to the regenerated program. I found no concrete or symbolic witness making a candidate rule prove a false result under the intended supplied-semantics interpretation. The remaining issue is narrower: the human-facing “ordinary decimal digits” meaning rests on an explicit, result-bearing external `Int2String` contract that is not universally proved inside K; I’m documenting that as a non-fatal trust-boundary concern.
